  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A marginal risk for severe storms and a slight\nrisk of enhanced rainfall remains for portions of the area this\nafternoon into this evening.\n\nA cold front will continue to move across the area this\nafternoon into this evening and overnight. Ahead of the cold\nfront, warm/humid air will remain in place across eastern New\nJersey, as well as central/southern Delaware and eastern\nMaryland, leading to CAPE values of 1000-2000 J/kg. As a mid- \nlevel/ short wave/vorticity impulse moves across the area this \nafternoon into tonight, showers and thunderstorms should \ndevelop across the area. With 1000-2000 J/kg of CAPE, along with\nshear values near 30-40 knots, a few strong to severe storms \nmay develop across portions of eastern New Jersey, as well as \ncentral/southern Delaware and eastern Maryland. With PW values \nin the 1.5-2.0 range, heavy rainfall will also remain possible.\n\nAfter tonight, the front will shift offshore, bringing the rain\nwith it. As high pressure builds in from the north, dry weather\nwill return through the much of the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A pair of systems will bring more thunderstorm \nchances early next week.\n\nAn open wave, mid level, short wave trough will be crossing \nthrough the Northeast on Sunday. Currently, we have relatively \nlow chances for precipitation with this trough, primarily \nbecause there is limited opportunity for moisture advection \nbefore this trough arrives. Additionally, at this point there \ndoesn't appear to be much in the way of boundary layer\nconvergence.\n\nThere is a different story with the next mid/upper level trough\napproaching our region Tuesday into Wednesday. For this system,\nthere will likely be ample opportunity for moisture advection\nMonday into Tuesday. Additionally, it is likely to be\naccompanied by a low and at least a cold front at the surface.\nThat being said, there is considerable differences between\nmodels on how this system will evolve. Some models are depicting\nthis as an open wave, fast moving trough with only a limited\nopportunity for rain. Others, most notably the ECMWF, depict the\nmid upper level trough becoming a closed low, and then slowing\nits eastward progress considerably. Given the large differences\nin uncertainty, forecast stays close to the blend of guidance\nthrough this period.",
